  "body": "Found by the new Windows check (Windows 11, Git for Windows defaults: `core.autocrlf=true`), main 380c29b3c, `cargo xtask gate quick`.\n\n## What happens\n\nGit for Windows checks text files out with CRLF by default. `.gitattributes` pins `crates/app/tests/golden/**`, tester kits, examples and `*.sh` to LF, but not every fixture that tests compare byte for byte or parse. Four suites (17 tests) fail on such a checkout, each within a second:\n\n| Suite | Reads | Pinned? |\n|---|---|---|\n| post `explain_fanuc_family`, `explain_remaining` | `crates/post/tests/golden/explain/*.nc` | **no** |\n| app `wire_taper_angle` | `crates/app/tests/edm/wire-iso-fixture.mch` (include_str) | **no** |\n| app `import_step_curve_guard` | `testdata/models/box.step` (include_str) | **no** |\n\nExample: `fanuc_explain_golden_preserves_program` - left (generated) ends lines with `\\n`, right (golden from the checkout) with `\\r\\n`; the content is otherwise identical.\n\n## Fix\n\nPin every byte-compared or parsed text fixture to LF, e.g.:\n\n```\ncrates/*/tests/golden/** text eol=lf\ncrates/*/tests/**/*.mch  text eol=lf\ntestdata/**/*.step       text eol=lf\ntestdata/**/*.stp        text eol=lf\n```\n\n(or one broader rule for `crates/*/tests/**` and `testdata/**`), then `git add --renormalize .`. A unit test in xtask (or a CI-free check) could assert that `git check-attr eol` is `lf` for every file under the golden/fixture dirs.\n\n## Done means\n\nOn a fresh Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`, the four suites above pass; `git check-attr eol -- <each fixture>` reports `lf`.\n",
